# Greetings: 200 first, 201 later, 202 has rented before, 203 after Gob got the back pay.
{200}{}{Hey, kid. New in town, or just lost? Room's a hundred and twenty, up front. Talk is free until I get bored.}
{201}{}{Hey, kid. Still breathing? Good for you. Need the room? Still a hundred and twenty.}
{202}{}{Back for the good bed? Hundred and twenty, same as before. I changed the sheet. Well. I turned it over.}
{203}{}{Gob told me what you did. Nobody ever handed us anything in this place but a bill. You want something from me, kid, you ask.}
{205}{}{What else, kid? The room's still a hundred and twenty.}
{206}{}{Something else.}

{210}{}{I need a room for the night.}
{211}{}{A hundred and twenty? For a bed?}
{212}{}{Tell me about the people in here.}
{214}{}{See you around, Nova.}
{215}{}{Me sleepy.}
{216}{}{Bye, pretty lady.}

{220}{}{Hundred and twenty, up front. Moriarty's price, not mine. You get the bed, the door and eight hours of nobody. Paying?}
{221}{}{Here. A hundred and twenty.}
{222}{}{Not tonight.}
{223}{}{[She counts it twice, for Moriarty, and tips her head at the back room.] Last door. Eight hours, and nobody comes through it but you. If you talk in your sleep, kid, I've heard worse from people who were awake.}
{224}{}{That's not a hundred and twenty. Moriarty counts it twice, kid, and he takes the shortages out of me. Come back when you've got all of it.}
{226}{}{[Go to bed.]}
# 227: the message window when the player wakes in the rented room.
{227}{}{Eight hours go by, and for once nothing tries to kill you. You wake in the back room of Moriarty's with every ache gone.}
{225}{}{For a bed, a door that shuts and nobody going through your pack while you sleep. Moriarty sets the price. I just say it out loud.}

{230}{}{Sure. It's slow. Who do you want to hear about?}
{231}{}{Gob.}
{232}{}{Moriarty.}
{233}{}{The man in the suit.}
# Gob: 240 alive, 241 dead.
{240}{}{Gob's the only decent man in this place, and half of him fell off years ago. Moriarty hits him when the till is short. Be nice to him, kid. It costs you nothing, and it's the only pay he gets.}
{241}{}{Gob was the one decent thing in this bar. Now there's nobody behind it saying sorry. I never thought I'd miss hearing it.}
{242}{}{He owns the bar, the beds, the debts and most of the secrets in town. He'll smile and call you friend. Count your fingers after.}
# Burke: 243 in his corner, 244 gone from the saloon, 245 dead, 246 shot Simms.
{243}{}{He's had that corner a month. Never touched a girl, never finished a drink. He watches the bomb through the door, the way some men watch a card game. Stay clear of that one, kid.}
{244}{}{Gone, and good riddance. He left the way a landlord leaves. Like he'd be back to collect.}
{245}{}{Dead. And not one person in this room asked where they put him. First time he ever fitted in here.}
{246}{}{He shot the sheriff in the back and walked out past me, close enough to touch. He said 'excuse me.' I still hear it.}

# Asking for the password: 250 Speech, 251 Charisma, 252 a paying guest, 253 after the back pay, 254 no leverage.
{250}{}{He'd never guess it came from you. What's his password?}
{251}{}{[Smile.] You'd tell me, though. Wouldn't you?}
{252}{}{I paid for the room. That makes me a regular. The password?}
{253}{}{I need the password to Moriarty's terminal.}
{254}{}{Moriarty's terminal. What's the password?}
{260}{}{[She glances at the office door, then says one word, low, against your ear.] He types it every morning, so he never forgets who he hates. You didn't hear it from me, kid.}
{261}{}{Kid, I like breathing. Give me a reason to like you more than that, and ask me again.}

# INT 3 and below.
{270}{}{Sleepy. Right. The bed costs a hundred and twenty coins, kid. All your fingers, twelve times over. Got that many?}
{271}{}{Me pay. [Give her $120.]}
{272}{}{Too many fingers.}
{273}{}{[She counts the coins off your fingers.] Bed's through there, big fella. Last door. You look like you sleep like a rock. A loud rock.}
{275}{}{Me sleep now.}
{274}{}{That's not enough, sweetheart. Go and find more coins. I'll keep the bed warm. Well. I'll keep the bed.}
